bfe-54ae-b334-1592fbad61b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-7582" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/80646a56ea6df17b6f0b6460f3d4918174966773b9e7910d252c9098af3b0f48.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ intersects the line $y=x-3$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we substitute $y=x-3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(x-3+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16+{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =10 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x+10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-7582/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-7582"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15805/selection/186b23b6228f1a93b6b56d7f1761d6983f6300e406730a9c3243a663821081ca" :answer/id #uuid "7186e710-e7d2-5f97-91b8-fc81553ade1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15805/selection/f55bf6c076a4a78f5124e581c45d0bac2bcfb3469097799a564f657b48724801" :answer/id #uuid "a1099d2b-058c-5dfe-8647-be9cabb1413e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-4,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15805/selection/9363bbda483a50e1e9185f5b456000fc8cb3905950d3d3314716264f41641333" :answer/id #uuid "ed6f3bfb-f40a-563e-8267-c86f1e878913" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15805/selection/783e61add4cce782f5d634d77404e7a8122064ecae24490f2cb02c904382667a" :answer/id #uuid "2ca4f91f-7eb1-5ef4-8200-3295055974eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15805/selection/6246a284e43cf02fea39b39a19c5b302c16ffcd9010a16b4c15316a4c7633375" :answer/id #uuid "74f35b7a-e710-5552-8eff-fdbd9fe09c8f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15805/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5fddc856-8795-517d-97c8-053c51c2231b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15805/selection/6246a284e43cf02fea39b39a19c5b302c16ffcd9010a16b4c15316a4c7633375" :answer-field/choices ["q-15805/selection/186b23b6228f1a93b6b56d7f1761d6983f6300e406730a9c3243a663821081ca" "q-15805/selection/f55bf6c076a4a78f5124e581c45d0bac2bcfb3469097799a564f657b48724801" "q-15805/selection/9363bbda483a50e1e9185f5b456000fc8cb3905950d3d3314716264f41641333" "q-15805/selection/783e61add4cce782f5d634d77404e7a8122064ecae24490f2cb02c904382667a" "q-15805/selection/6246a284e43cf02fea39b39a19c5b302c16ffcd9010a16b4c15316a4c7633375"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15805" :question/id #uuid "cfa05974-d33e-5f3c-90f3-bf0b329bfc3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15805" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/de12c0b5be8318cc21c62615d24125998c22d55d37abafdee37c103c3840cb71.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ intersects the line $x-3y=2$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4)}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{10}{3})}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+10 & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-4,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-4,-2).$ Next, we plug in $x=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=2$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ at the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-15805/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15805"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-14665/selection/0345762bc9aa4535038b7e88d4c2c3f003f5c09305a9aa023868d9c077ca9ca4" :answer/id #uuid "e8de84de-b9d8-5e93-90ee-43c6ed8c923d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(6,10)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14665/selection/d7ad5e447c6bb70aabced684aba1eeff53db720fcce97940f929b9658072fe3b" :answer/id #uuid "57f97ee3-8db2-5be9-ae63-04b75e095330" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(5,9)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14665/selection/23b7313fb8d7a9d625b65f6dd49788227c7737b30ba2e612b51076905ada9a39" :answer/id #uuid "ff23c307-f70c-56f9-99bb-4da66f8107a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(5,9)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14665/selection/a57ceade17cd957c4a12748c7f4d360cf014ab907f9260ce866dd74732c05a0a" :answer/id #uuid "56e5ff07-edff-524d-ac7d-6b2441247823" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1.5,1.5)$ and $(5,10)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14665/selection/e14463f9edd898fe1e3248a50140ac887325ee63d4da0b78637a894208558918" :answer/id #uuid "ca3ee1cd-f431-5899-992b-fcb78ec1d787" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(6,10)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14665/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "54e92426-001f-5226-9eca-1501c36f8bd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-14665/selection/0345762bc9aa4535038b7e88d4c2c3f003f5c09305a9aa023868d9c077ca9ca4" :answer-field/choices ["q-14665/selection/0345762bc9aa4535038b7e88d4c2c3f003f5c09305a9aa023868d9c077ca9ca4" "q-14665/selection/d7ad5e447c6bb70aabced684aba1eeff53db720fcce97940f929b9658072fe3b" "q-14665/selection/23b7313fb8d7a9d625b65f6dd49788227c7737b30ba2e612b51076905ada9a39" "q-14665/selection/a57ceade17cd957c4a12748c7f4d360cf014ab907f9260ce866dd74732c05a0a" "q-14665/selection/e14463f9edd898fe1e3248a50140ac887325ee63d4da0b78637a894208558918"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14665" :question/id #uuid "249a4827-1fab-5d89-acce-30cbdc7d02ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-14665" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/8c9f433d2e23db9dc8abb81395e91acf59047e26221b1218393c4905548d1f35.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=34$ intersects the line $y=x+4$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=x+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} & =34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(x+4-5)^{2} & =34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(x-1)^{2} & =34 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =34 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x-24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-2,6$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\  & =-2+4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-2,2).$ Next, we plug in $x=6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\  & =6+4 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(6,10).$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x+4$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-5)^{2}=34$ at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(6,10).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-14665/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14665"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117247/selection/701d9edf4389163bf7f7005e41cfbdea75231c1cb8161e11ea2534dd4b187ca9" :answer/id #uuid "d3a566e7-ffa5-5ec6-9fc0-2a252b40996d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(3,\\frac{7}{3})$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117247/selection/503fcb45d5a6f906eb66710edb2ae8469646e8cf81e02d26bc2c8ef10463f56e" :answer/id #uuid "549f7dc4-8b52-5bca-9629-1e67e6233db3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$ and $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117247/selection/1437162c723066e6042c49a51554ab4e4d6522aa9a717b0e3d4439b76a9ed60c" :answer/id #uuid "85826fff-b2b3-5574-871e-fcc02e875021" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(3,\\frac{7}{3})$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117247/selection/089a77266ef0b1f8f75e0bc99b1f28568b9213f6049607cfbd1fdc511c048125" :answer/id #uuid "9ee5a54a-bfba-5cfa-b0a4-267b6ecd3c54" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{6})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117247/selection/6b9373a2c16795ac752c8f9e9c505ab998084fb06c08af42a00a5f2fe4b885ee" :answer/id #uuid "d90d92ef-71e0-5265-aa9c-ceff1cb328d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117247/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ececc18-1ac1-5cc4-865d-259fc6da1ed1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117247/selection/6b9373a2c16795ac752c8f9e9c505ab998084fb06c08af42a00a5f2fe4b885ee" :answer-field/choices ["q-117247/selection/701d9edf4389163bf7f7005e41cfbdea75231c1cb8161e11ea2534dd4b187ca9" "q-117247/selection/503fcb45d5a6f906eb66710edb2ae8469646e8cf81e02d26bc2c8ef10463f56e" "q-117247/selection/1437162c723066e6042c49a51554ab4e4d6522aa9a717b0e3d4439b76a9ed60c" "q-117247/selection/089a77266ef0b1f8f75e0bc99b1f28568b9213f6049607cfbd1fdc511c048125" "q-117247/selection/6b9373a2c16795ac752c8f9e9c505ab998084fb06c08af42a00a5f2fe4b885ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117247" :question/id #uuid "f9516424-4bd5-5885-82ea-4588d28a4cdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117247" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/5180dfe04f31f43fdcdfa8bd720951df0c6fb0d73cd2db3912b37e172f714131.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $x-3y=-4$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=-4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{8x}{9}+\\frac{16}{9} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}+\\frac{25}{9} & =\\frac{45}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{10x}{9}-\\frac{20}{9} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-1,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-1,1).$ Next, we plug in $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=-4$ intersects the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the points $(2,2)$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-117247/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117247"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-7579/selection/08a4e19ea91ba8137700f187faf6245713d06baaeefe9f0eb99b7205b92df11b" :answer/id #uuid "a23e20ae-b0e6-51ad-a4d2-1ea5441d6419" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-3$ and $4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7579/selection/cbf92c0c6edc5a58bf3327780a286a9f6e5603e437b1e36a1eae658557b5b012" :answer/id #uuid "2e9e7741-5cb9-5871-b74c-8dc80d4c8b6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2.5$ and $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7579/selection/003ad58c1c236ce40a6f2165c2b645dd8e9d24bc7b7b9eaaf6d20bfa53e10536" :answer/id #uuid "8c143dc5-e2d5-5f65-9325-5fab419e19e4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2$ and $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7579/selection/c9a5e7043448f0f21f2cf1e36ecfe3942d49816f218190eea3884d49a46c2f83" :answer/id #uuid "da51350b-9f61-5aec-8cbd-ab28c1254987" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2.5$ and $3.5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-7579/selection/4eaa012edf1ad76580b986dd1e9c625619d051b1a4a84515