q-78512" :question/id #uuid "8c7d9d94-79b2-54cd-80f6-31a00c58749c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78512" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/d6f30f0e475fc222dc54c4e7f04bc3f48442b8430c3b830bfd7d11a5d6d788e3.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y-2x=-11$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $y-2x=-11,$ as follows: \n\n$$\ny=2x-11\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=2x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x-11)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+4{x}^{2}-44x+121 & =5 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-50x+130 & =5 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-50x+125 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+25 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-5 & =0 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-11 \\\\  & =2(5)-11 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y-2x=-11$ intersects the circle $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ at the point $(5,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78512/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78512"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117542/selection/1ebdf60e5cc50efefa0f0bbc926690139a6c732a1a8226e561104581503f9fbd" :answer/id #uuid "7dfc237b-0391-53f4-9e07-18741e9ef4be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117542/selection/18e5a8fd029cad6f2b4c7cf6d1d9ba8fc9cbf7ced3134e3510a92db76c173cef" :answer/id #uuid "6b73fd24-5d1d-549f-ab0b-97f073f6002e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117542/selection/6297ef1bbad17a42f720256f720c74911c7019be1e259a972499280d3dc12eca" :answer/id #uuid "92277082-f9fa-5699-9813-eeb338f3ff98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1.5,-6.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117542/selection/7c881a3130cf5ba5f4d7ce51471c0dd9d4b2414dcab4e4043ff888e5db7c5fd3" :answer/id #uuid "f1be655d-1d80-5110-9c7d-43bb4301d17d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117542/selection/659c85abed9e76787f05c7595af40253179018ed47eed339adf234db0061a3cf" :answer/id #uuid "092f5a5c-6a4c-5ad7-b107-25c85dafb8db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2.5,-3.5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117542/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f96ad68-0d47-56ff-a4ec-bb8b1a61ea5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117542/selection/18e5a8fd029cad6f2b4c7cf6d1d9ba8fc9cbf7ced3134e3510a92db76c173cef" :answer-field/choices ["q-117542/selection/1ebdf60e5cc50efefa0f0bbc926690139a6c732a1a8226e561104581503f9fbd" "q-117542/selection/18e5a8fd029cad6f2b4c7cf6d1d9ba8fc9cbf7ced3134e3510a92db76c173cef" "q-117542/selection/6297ef1bbad17a42f720256f720c74911c7019be1e259a972499280d3dc12eca" "q-117542/selection/7c881a3130cf5ba5f4d7ce51471c0dd9d4b2414dcab4e4043ff888e5db7c5fd3" "q-117542/selection/659c85abed9e76787f05c7595af40253179018ed47eed339adf234db0061a3cf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117542" :question/id #uuid "67a9b2cd-1787-5032-a8c9-13cecd77788f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117542" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/a3a9751a19c9a853c425b81ce63ccb76b4cd4350f152df3dbe00a1cdb0f8cdab.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=-3x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-11+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-10)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+90 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-3$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-11 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-11 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ at the point $(-3,-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-117542/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117542"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-7585"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/f17e4718742158f65db70bd25e0ba97fd0fba793b5212eee0ee60072eb4231e9.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\ny=x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8$ at the point $(-2,-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-117546/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "6307667c-c334-55b8-8947-075a4896981c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117546/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "c24e1bac-3c95-597a-9c23-651cb8ce5d6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117546/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "2a9df10d-0406-5afa-8406-2bfc116d2091" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117546/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "3803a30f-e30d-5793-bbc5-96e08b0ae9df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117546/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "73641b57-7ccc-5781-bbb1-059986ebf422" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117546/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a61f814-3085-5e3e-982f-b82601f07526" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117546/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-117546/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-117546/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-117546/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-117546/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-117546/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117546" :question/id #uuid "90e9bd05-dbac-514e-ba2a-1c98522457da" :question/math-academy-id "q-117546" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/91ead412d3e333fd4bfbcce785813a530e66ca39858ca108e9920d349443bb53.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point where the line $-2x+3y=12$ intersects the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=13$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $-2x+3y=12,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x}{3}+4\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{2x}{3}+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+4+1)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+5)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{3}+25 & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{26x}{3}+13 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117546/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117546"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15806/selection/4ec4dbda2081a25eb39e5c54be41fffc44e2d0516a4b93b95619181850a8f9ad" :answer/id #uuid "8494e28e-c816-530d-a46e-8c2f51dd4277" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15806/selection/c6a1f0b35fb86f12a0e1138b6e2c0461f265df1c7e058c326ad8966bfd682852" :answer/id #uuid "252af879-fed6-5806-95fd-54362544d1fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15806/selection/265033e705ddbe9f1f30f47188b03a2b28b2a12a3ed7f75cd554e6baee2550bc" :answer/id #uuid "9c968461-bf24-53a6-854e-2ed083bb1c8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15806/selection/a7821a28970c89039ed3e20edba9a43f2ffcfea2c09f937084393b648799df9d" :answer/id #uuid "37f9290c-5e0b-565b-b93d-d8acf994d262" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15806/selection/66e9706a41786654138bad54e9f9465d1eb13af1723fccd92995cfc8619107f7" :answer/id #uuid "7610e9d2-4bfd-5e3b-8abf-8f5efebb4349" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15806/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b14fa81e-6252-56bd-ba2d-872f75be5089" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15806/selection/265033e705ddbe9f1f30f47188b03a2b28b2a12a3ed7f75cd554e6baee2550bc" :answer-field/choices ["q-15806/selection/4ec4dbda2081a25eb39e5c54be41fffc44e2d0516a4b93b95619181850a8f9ad" "q-15806/selection/c6a1f0b35fb86f12a0e1138b6e2c0461f265df1c7e058c326ad8966bfd682852" "q-15806/selection/265033e705ddbe9f1f30f47188b03a2b28b2a12a3ed7f75cd554e6baee2550bc" "q-15806/selection/a7821a28970c89039ed3e20edba9a43f2ffcfea2c09f937084393b648799df9d" "q-15806/selection/66e9706a41786654138bad54e9f9465d1eb13af1723fccd92995cfc8619107f7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15806" :question/id #uuid "df3cd888-e784-5638-9690-b7ff43d4a76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-15806" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/43fe48b1980d01aa9dbb7cc0f614272ad19e7cfe47b5a5a83a0f81e7539f285b.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=10$ intersects the line $3y-x=-7.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $3y-x=-7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}+5)}^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{16x}{9}+\\frac{64}{9} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+10 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $3y-x=-7$ intersects the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=10$ at the point $(1,-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-15806/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff002e26-f100-5b44-9175-a363d0808f9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15806"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117734/selection/01cf884d946368d2afc6f3c97a9e15b707e07218f2898fbe36d38bd86a8567a1" :answer/id #uuid "82379688-2b6e-5b92-8d47-74542f278bd7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-3$ and $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117734/selection/eec3cb16a9e054dca65fe9b6fae8dc5c46b1e08dc97e74f81ecc28238c8e23b2" :answer/id #uuid "933645e4-7426-5e55-85c4-26bb808dab00" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2$ and $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117734/selection/6b341a7aac56f14fd202e2388a7faabe2aa301f4412f8dfc71058439e74f26c6" :answer/id #uuid "457da568-a1b7-5aef-bc5c-199e6b40d3a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-1$ and $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117734/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" :answer/id #uuid "fd682f0b-f0a5-5698-831b-22adcc338263" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117734/selection/11f3ac18494484f8b3500d7ab98b2ef765a9fba113415c5e058be57093fc0c7d" :answer/id #uuid "f55058c9-58fd-5b86-8bfa-1216f657b136" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117734/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "13942aed-5583-5808-af56-0712bdc58712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117734/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" :answer-field/choices ["q-117734/selection/01cf884d946368d2afc6f3c97a9e15b707e07218f2898fbe36d38bd86a8567a1" "q-117734/selection/eec3cb16a9e054dca65fe9b6fae8dc5c46b1e08dc97e74f81ecc28238c8e23b2" "q-117734/selection/6b341a7aac56f14fd202e2388a7faabe2aa301f4412f8dfc71058439e74f26c6" "q-117734/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" "q-117734/selection/11f3ac18494484f8b3500d7ab98b2ef765a9fba113415c5e058be57093fc0c7d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117734" :question/id #uuid "ea101bc1-4f0a-50d4-b42a-0a6309938246" :question/math-academy-id "q-117734" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1$ intersects the line $y=-3x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-3x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-3x+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+9{x}^{2}-6x+1 & =1 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 10\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $10{x}^{2}-4x+1=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $y=-3x.$ \n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/87f75953dbe795a8e5db201778b4ff3127f02bae30ef3b8e8ffa7bd7956c1bc3.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117734/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117734"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-54242/selection/a304f7c7f8a4c6ff9ac349ee42c59819aa98d58679d84bb1afe66aa4ace41878" :answer/id #uuid "693b09a3-2021-5f03-82b7-612a6fe8cf87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-1$ and $4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-54242/selection/c0cdde82342237d57eece7e4773614ba7ad9a9111644679a3f13791e099f52e7" :answer/id #uuid "a092a625-5df4-5dfa-886c-5cbda375a4ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-54242/selection/4547119d2d649db801809f051027dc10728908c676b90c2e8e16635046a227c9" :answer/id #uuid "485296a9-ecec-55d5-a585-0bef558c41cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ and $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-54242/selection/37d35deefcfb6f2fab086f5dc4fee6e30ac4b8770cd32ecf34c423466965a1f3" :answer/id #uuid "e4f47b2e-c021-5309-a9c5-e845ed59476f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-3$ and $-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-54242/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" :answer/id #uuid "e6bb7d67-b60d-5d5b-b126-db3d8e2a35f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-54242/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "20a20482-4a16-5f59-941c-b41061fd7257" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-54242/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" :answer-field/choices ["q-54242/selection/a304f7c7f8a4c6ff9ac349ee42c59819aa98d58679d84bb1afe66aa4ace41878" "q-54242/selection/c0cdde82342237d57eece7e4773614ba7ad9a9111644679a3f13791e099f52e7" "q-54242/selection/4547119d2d649db801809f051027dc10728908c676b90c2e8e16635046a227c9" "q-54242/selection/37d35deefcfb6f2fab086f5dc4fee6e30ac4b8770cd32ecf34c423466965a1f3" "q-54242/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-54242" :question/id #uuid "6de12d86-c297-540f-8fcc-949f18bd056f" :question/math-academy-id "q-54242" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ intersects the line $y=-x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-x+3)^{2} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{x}^{2}-6x+9 & =5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $2{x}^{2}-4x+5=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ does not intersect the line $y=-x+3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/85c9bea054f2e246d62864b022e53a6f1d23886b0f0c9f8c80c8e0a5b1be9cd8.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-54242/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-54242"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78651/selection/cb959eeaaa021c0fae80ac76a325b3ec116f1c27166535d0913c0e9dfb9ef30a" :answer/id #uuid "b999e9a8-1f8f-5099-a510-4b9555426023" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-1.5$ and $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78651/selection/117ead39f73b7ff643bee6f97273a121f5aff102c3b11f6ce496aa96b017a60c" :answer/id #uuid "1046217e-9437-5289-8209-b079a3ed3e21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-4$ and $-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78651/selection/9d24149fae7ed3c8ded173362d1185a392e9450a68167efe583749113804f5b7" :answer/id #uuid "5496e9de-7ea6-5674-ba15-be502b995ef3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78651/selection/33208b17e5dc52874c0ac529a1b9fb2c7d9896f4b1632bcab93173e8e3dff689" :answer/id #uuid "fc0c9670-1389-5aab-8bfb-da2fb7db4d95" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-4$ and $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78651/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" :answer/id #uuid "b509a1e3-8b23-516a-853b-2cc4c5616ae0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78651/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "62d5c5b0-c924-5b7f-8b0a-4854761b0596" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78651/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" :answer-field/choices ["q-78651/selection/cb959eeaaa021c0fae80ac76a325b3ec116f1c27166535d0913c0e9dfb9ef30a" "q-78651/selection/117ead39f73b7ff643bee6f97273a121f5aff102c3b11f6ce496aa96b017a60c" "q-78651/selection/9d24149fae7ed3c8ded173362d1185a392e9450a68167efe583749113804f5b7" "q-78651/selection/33208b17e5dc52874c0ac529a1b9fb2c7d9896f4b1632bcab93173e8e3dff689" "q-78651/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78651" :question/id #uuid "4e85f65e-e9ff-5ffd-af20-284e643c5763" :question/math-academy-id "q-78651" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ intersects the line $2x-y=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $2x-y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=2x+3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(2x+3-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(2x+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+4{x}^{2}+4x+1 & =4 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+12x+13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={12}^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =144-260 \\\\  & =-116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-116<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $5{x}^{2}+12x+13=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle $(x+4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ does not intersect the line $2x-y=-3$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/00bc159e9c25b913454c6037a822e41931c6224f3e6f0180da550fe9c857e718.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-78651/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78651"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117737/selection/819a757d4e0bc485722090a78c1e2f2785f28af835c17515ba5670f2d7cae63c" :answer/id #uuid "1d4549cb-c476-521e-9ec0-1fdcd890b7eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-2$ and $4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117737/selection/2f817eb15f994caf46175717f58bb0f51c9b451dbd74a973fe420ad3a98f96f2" :answer/id #uuid "aaddf264-1d25-5a7b-b2be-f5563dc7bad2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117737/selection/6c5183b804165499a69b40f6090f66f1a122a0207e8bac4bf82d49bc4ef63202" :answer/id #uuid "1d1a878c-7f2f-5c35-bf28-7b0cf8af73a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117737/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" :answer/id #uuid "65de35fb-0f08-5436-9a3c-c6f3c99f492f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117737/selection/7723f1eebc074448fc359e9bfa8644efe89ff6a0b4d43df92838c4e699c1926c" :answer/id #uuid "10fefbb7-794d-58e2-a2c3-611ff4449143" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-1$ and $3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117737/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2963a972-c781-52d2-a755-4aa69c211d09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117737/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" :answer-field/choices ["q-117737/selection/819a757d4e0bc485722090a78c1e2f2785f28af835c17515ba5670f2d7cae63c" "q-117737/selection/2f817eb15f994caf46175717f58bb0f51c9b451dbd74a973fe420ad3a98f96f2" "q-117737/selection/6c5183b804165499a69b40f6090f66f1a122a0207e8bac4bf82d49bc4ef63202" "q-117737/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" "q-117737/selection/7723f1eebc074448fc359e9bfa8644efe89ff6a0b4d43df92838c4e699c1926c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117737" :question/id #uuid "61ffdc01-8414-55ad-b1b4-42608bbe9957" :question/math-academy-id "q-117737" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ intersects the line $x+2y=4$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x+2y=4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2}x+2)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+4 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={2}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4-15 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-11<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $x+2y=4$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/400b997f9f1e83d6d2dd6c34b68a01ad4de5b352e40de2401eb516ca181f3163.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-117737/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117737"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78654/selection/2a3abcba3e3452a1cddb73086c6185423d6f0ee1d37446493f980d00de480a48" :answer/id #uuid "32c1fbf7-f99c-513f-a695-fd26537f157c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-1$ and $-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78654/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" :answer/id #uuid "a3df063b-9205-5f0e-b72b-66f776a24421" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The circle and the line do not intersect" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78654/selection/952c65d3525b53f428134a8466e351432ff36696eaf38052c226fbd88b03b8d5" :answer/id #uuid "c5cfe886-10e4-5718-a993-c95f75626d46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78654/selection/14e8c173db4fa8e66662f59f07b99485e7f2510c26934c04afdb2f6cad6f1b3f" :answer/id #uuid "42923d41-651f-5905-b4c1-3103c3bbc634" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$-1$ and $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78654/selection/001f9ce039520c79c53626128ee97492af07892bf6dc478a391944098e441f63" :answer/id #uuid "e0cbed9a-76fc-5516-abfa-21e54e5f6073" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ and $4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78654/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "34bd55fe-b38d-5c67-92d4-c5e35befbbe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78654/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" :answer-field/choices ["q-78654/selection/2a3abcba3e3452a1cddb73086c6185423d6f0ee1d37446493f980d00de480a48" "q-78654/selection/7cc8f05badf00d62932d514fa5b64af5d0ba9b74cbf4e644d847d480b71f175a" "q-78654/selection/952c65d3525b53f428134a8466e351432ff36696eaf38052c226fbd88b03b8d5" "q-78654/selection/14e8c173db4fa8e66662f59f07b99485e7f2510c26934c04afdb2f6cad6f1b3f" "q-78654/selection/001f9ce039520c79c53626128ee97492af07892bf6dc478a391944098e441f63"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78654" :question/id #uuid "124f9d08-ccb9-500f-a740-588c701739e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-78654" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2$ intersects the line $y=2x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=2x+1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x+1-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(2x-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+9+4{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =2 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-10x+8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-10)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =100-160 \\\\  & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-60<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $5{x}^{2}-10x+8=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2$ does not intersect the line $y=2x+1.$ \n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/56e313584d7de2f1c40302135b00ef80a1d3c1e920407c9c4a0c62c3c6769456.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78654/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d0e48e3-39ca-551a-80a4-dc7ff7855197"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78654"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117859/selection/8c4cc0d74e46339f07aa492d4ae4ccd261209f7f0a850a551cbf1fe6e021d68c" :answer/id #uuid "e4b7a98a-d153-5eb2-bd71-ca1abaeb61a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2\\sqrt{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117859/selection/e43e2f6cb89390c2e57c826e0cc82bf6d6a07e66a3059426ccd53727befd93ae" :answer/id #uuid "5e2a6057-7c59-52a6-845f-800695f604fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117859/selection/848d88a1686f2280b66a10f764d8d2e96b6a86804a638ea7987540baf5e98416" :answer/id #uuid "0bfaf101-903d-5e7f-9c9b-af5acb098a09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117859/selection/28eabfb927d0051daa8871332c9ee81769eb1c35f65800ca0db4ae083b1453e3" :answer/id #uuid "67763a38-60d4-5ce2-bfb1-77d3fba66510" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}=13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117859/selection/aa0f57cf22b5d2f2e2794ae444253a95c85a9690acd889a06983c99a38223814" :answer/id #uuid "222bf662-e011-58d2-9a31-4c8b4450328f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117859/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ed40211-7a4a-5fdc-89f4-a0a73aa76dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117859/selection/848d88a1686f2280b66a10f764d8d2e96b6a86804a638ea7987540baf5e98416" :answer-field/choices ["q-117859/selection/8c4cc0d74e46339f07aa492d4ae4ccd261209f7f0a850a551cbf1fe6e021d68c" "q-117859/selection/e43e2f6cb89390c2e57c826e0cc82bf6d6a07e66a3059426ccd53727befd93ae" "q-117859/selection/848d88a1686f2280b66a10f764d8d2e96b6a86804a638ea7987540baf5e98416" "q-117859/selection/28eabfb927d0051daa8871332c9ee81769eb1c35f65800ca0db4ae083b1453e3" "q-117859/selection/aa0f57cf22b5d2f2e2794ae444253a95c85a9690acd889a06983c99a38223814"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117859" :question/id #uuid "ac7dcd31-21b4-5064-a3d7-5c55f9b204d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117859" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/c100b27df3e4d9b23846961ad5d168fbe6f0eecd94ab8a9b99f51e82bca064a3.png)\n\nThe line $2y-3x=-24$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-3x & =-24 \\\\ -3x & =-24 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3(0) & =-24 \\\\ 2y & =-24 \\\\ y & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(8,0)$ and $(0,-12)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8+0}{2},\\frac{0-12}{2})=(4,-6).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(4,-6)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(4-8)}^{2}+(-6-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(4,-6)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{13},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-6))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{13})}^{2} \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2} & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117859/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117859"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117857/selection/9c5ee587a19bc67285d02fa1fa4a67254d3ffb96f7962c3b9c2d4f6f10ddf324" :answer/id #uuid "d5755761-db97-5922-a440-b069e501e002" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=2\\sqrt{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117857/selection/44eab0c32a551abaa551d0776b859269a38b13b35ca9af8bbf31173bdec7ab55" :answer/id #uuid "36d03c1e-0297-5df9-be70-7a899b50d645" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=68" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117857/selection/1d253531b99e30b83df2456f42d84f0d8aba85642ba9c57a5643e8679faca787" :answer/id #uuid "59548f33-3e11-584e-ad31-7a7410835c2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+8)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117857/selection/90f62f6b713374080ae7f43e054b91be895c8f9224ee850a1805c511e3f913fa" :answer/id #uuid "f34bb61f-428d-52ba-87f5-f2806044c96c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117857/selection/e18806e7431092007d06e2cf0d1e7db762fc52778384e8fc9f201755a6d003dd" :answer/id #uuid "ec1b8649-14f1-5ce5-821e-a2e725b06541" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+8)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=68" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117857/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7802fa3-d135-5961-8d50-b8a9a452ee00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117857/selection/90f62f6b713374080ae7f43e054b91be895c8f9224ee850a1805c511e3f913fa" :answer-field/choices ["q-117857/selection/9c5ee587a19bc67285d02fa1fa4a67254d3ffb96f7962c3b9c2d4f6f10ddf324" "q-117857/selection/44eab0c32a551abaa551d0776b859269a38b13b35ca9af8bbf31173bdec7ab55" "q-117857/selection/1d253531b99e30b83df2456f42d84f0d8aba85642ba9c57a5643e8679faca787" "q-117857/selection/90f62f6b713374080ae7f43e054b91be895c8f9224ee850a1805c511e3f913fa" "q-117857/selection/e18806e7431092007d06e2cf0d1e7db762fc52778384e8fc9f201755a6d003dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117857" :question/id #uuid "910a6090-0381-577c-8b6e-114eac566776" :question/math-academy-id "q-117857" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/51d811f0b628a1854542d85954c26ca4658602cfcf7c207fbcfdb41115933139.png)\n\nThe line $4y+x=-8$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)+x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-0 & =-8 \\\\ 4y & =-8 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(-8,0)$ and $(0,-2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{-8+0}{2},\\frac{0-2}{2})=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-4,-1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(-8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(-4+8)}^{2}+(-1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-4,-1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{17},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-(-4))}^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{17})}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117857/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117857"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78784/selection/531daccbea95d209a74d90ac91f017611956eabc734534db8d6261197540345b" :answer/id #uuid "85730343-d77e-5bc8-9fd3-b80e643c2569" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78784/selection/01dc8c40eb3a930033c532e2854606a46f8fe8f27073f4628b19c9fe09d12b53" :answer/id #uuid "a306257d-786b-553b-862a-f324038468e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-2)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78784/selection/4fcbe70726759ebbc38bc98390da8c8e98416d566bbf25a1972610e820605b60" :answer/id #uuid "f9cb0df4-6354-578a-b329-a87b54f96c61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-2)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78784/selection/2a07ecf2925399746df6be3a3083dd4066eedd343f4104315555feff019c060f" :answer/id #uuid "33c247eb-27a8-5c0d-b61e-8dfc07ced165" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-2)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78784/selection/b0473831b7c61105640d1c27c537264c77763b336db93d76c583977e94dd0f00" :answer/id #uuid "29bbf5c1-2ed7-5b9c-9cf4-70bf3f3a40c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78784/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "78b690eb-9273-5652-a783-e0dbc69058ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78784/selection/b0473831b7c61105640d1c27c537264c77763b336db93d76c583977e94dd0f00" :answer-field/choices ["q-78784/selection/531daccbea95d209a74d90ac91f017611956eabc734534db8d6261197540345b" "q-78784/selection/01dc8c40eb3a930033c532e2854606a46f8fe8f27073f4628b19c9fe09d12b53" "q-78784/selection/4fcbe70726759ebbc38bc98390da8c8e98416d566bbf25a1972610e820605b60" "q-78784/selection/2a07ecf2925399746df6be3a3083dd4066eedd343f4104315555feff019c060f" "q-78784/selection/b0473831b7c61105640d1c27c537264c77763b336db93d76c583977e94dd0f00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78784" :question/id #uuid "6a7d9a2f-2a4c-5379-aee8-ef76e2496ac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-78784" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/36e373800d2bd917e38bec197fbb5add2ae785270c68ddb4daf740ad678d4ece.png)\n\nThe line $x+y=2$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & =2 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+y & =2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the  coordinates $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(1,1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(1,1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(2,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{(1-2)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(1,1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{2},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78784/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78784"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-117851/selection/67e91da0d3326b02bd50084237d012b53298c17e40d2c1ddc6103136afa878a0" :answer/id #uuid "30aa4d61-dbef-5ea3-805d-e03d06a6fe07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117851/selection/57380824b1c9d6570e8f5e01c460278b147a077264b14e5294c36f14381833e3" :answer/id #uuid "165c41bd-ae4e-5a9e-b357-7a44063862ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117851/selection/71b65c741348c1de0f0ff0f31793cac164ca78a6c34762ac33f22c0429bbd327" :answer/id #uuid "85ec250e-80f3-506a-b8ee-8fe0c0de78c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117851/selection/c174a2dd7b01a8927d7466e33ff492b9d7f5be3de939b283c6ba5890dc3da80a" :answer/id #uuid "924b6a3a-5195-5a29-91f4-28c623f33f25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2\\sqrt{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117851/selection/5e18016414a757584d9ccb67135a30a7b0415b3f5a26235c637c282be9df6b44" :answer/id #uuid "857b251c-9e0d-5097-b424-f99ecc32cbdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117851/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "50bc64f8-b568-5737-bf3a-4901e1a75286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-117851/selection/57380824b1c9d6570e8f5e01c460278b147a077264b14e5294c36f14381833e3" :answer-field/choices ["q-117851/selection/67e91da0d3326b02bd50084237d012b53298c17e40d2c1ddc6103136afa878a0" "q-117851/selection/57380824b1c9d6570e8f5e01c460278b147a077264b14e5294c36f14381833e3" "q-117851/selection/71b65c741348c1de0f0ff0f31793cac164ca78a6c34762ac33f22c0429bbd327" "q-117851/selection/c174a2dd7b01a8927d7466e33ff492b9d7f5be3de939b283c6ba5890dc3da80a" "q-117851/selection/5e18016414a757584d9ccb67135a30a7b0415b3f5a26235c637c282be9df6b44"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117851" :question/id #uuid "2c7a8305-48d6-5002-af81-74f86e2adf20" :question/math-academy-id "q-117851" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/0e5d6c9ce3198b5950c8a5396cf5500f4bddd232fbe44613611873c294dda6ea.png)\n\nThe line $3y+x=12$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+x & =12 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-0 & =12 \\\\ 3y & =12 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(12,0)$ and $(0,4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{12+0}{2},\\frac{0+4}{2})=(6,2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(6,2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(12,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(6-12)}^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-6)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(6,2)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{10},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-6)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{10})}^{2} \\\\ (x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-117851/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117851"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-14670/selection/d8751e7644b8a865117e73373ee760af0b743d9e1f7cb2766108d7c66a331fe1" :answer/id #uuid "5c757116-1504-5ffc-94d0-49f889371b78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14670/selection/55c89b43dc879814989ad5bb60ddd06c362f2934c7bd1432b2df1b0b6cc9b5c2" :answer/id #uuid "eada94d4-ae4c-52ab-a34c-861691c9edd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14670/selection/671f8ee3c9d736a52df70441e47a0ff84127dc39aa8bbcb0bb07423f077ae5c1" :answer/id #uuid "23d3947b-a3b6-5250-b92b-c40bc7923269" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14670/selection/bd6907570617cb8365f7c9bdbc6d4a6841a037034de8a29d671b47b0d3722cd8" :answer/id #uuid "30b95d83-3aa6-5d0b-a249-b1e0a9c2f7b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14670/selection/bd7e9ee081f316555f98c1da63245b09cab408cf41b6be0350cc2eef8227c2fd" :answer/id #uuid "4dc01482-028d-58f2-a3fa-ec8e6afea8f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14670/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9d5e498-4274-57b8-a27d-0a83df00dfdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-14670/selection/d8751e7644b8a865117e73373ee760af0b743d9e1f7cb2766108d7c66a331fe1" :answer-field/choices ["q-14670/selection/d8751e7644b8a865117e73373ee760af0b743d9e1f7cb2766108d7c66a331fe1" "q-14670/selection/55c89b43dc879814989ad5bb60ddd06c362f2934c7bd1432b2df1b0b6cc9b5c2" "q-14670/selection/671f8ee3c9d736a52df70441e47a0ff84127dc39aa8bbcb0bb07423f077ae5c1" "q-14670/selection/bd6907570617cb8365f7c9bdbc6d4a6841a037034de8a29d671b47b0d3722cd8" "q-14670/selection/bd7e9ee081f316555f98c1da63245b09cab408cf41b6be0350cc2eef8227c2fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14670" :question/id #uuid "83e94835-de37-5d69-b0fd-aa884e963d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14670" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/d627d54450733af4faf74d55497064a4e6ffdc958601862cc49886988f7448c4.png)\n\nThe line $y=2x-4$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & =y \\\\ y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(2,0)$ and $(0,-4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{0-4}{2})=(1,-2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(1,-2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(2,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(1-2)}^{2}+(-2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(1,-2)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{5},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-14670/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14670"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6761"] :question/problem "Find the points where the line $y = x +5$ intersects the circle $(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y = x + 5$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(x+5)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+{x}^{2}+10x+25 & =9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+14x+29 & =9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+14x+20 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute each of $x = -2$ and $x=-5$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+5 \\\\  & =-2+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-2,3).$\n\n- Substituting $x=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+5 \\\\  & =-5+5 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an intersection point $(-5,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $y = x + 5$ intersects the circle $(x+2)^2 + y^2 = 9$ at the points $(-2,3)$ and $(-5,0).$\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/9298e1af177eb9ba1702af0053c03d1d0e696819bae717c4892efdcd574c78fc.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3234"] :question/problem "Find the point where the line $y = -x + 3$ intersects the circle $(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y = -x + 3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+((-x+3)-2)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(-x+1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9+{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x+10 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+5 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to plug in $x = -1$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+3 \\\\  & =-(-1)+3 \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y = -x + 3$ intersects the circle $(x+3)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 8$ at the point $(-1,4).$\n\nA sketch of the circle and the line is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/90f7e5f187ad79e18d36ce253c8f1f998bc5a92790909f4840c84789c2ae5222.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3235"] :question/problem "Find the points where the circle $(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3$ intersects the line $y = 3x - 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y = 3x - 1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x$, if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+((3x-1)+1)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(3x)^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+9{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 10\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{D} = -24 < 0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $10x^2 - 4x + 1 = 0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x-2)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 3$ does not intercept the line $y = 3x - 1$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/590fa0a3fb8cb0e80c46859ad19b9457528c40f79f1ba33edea217d8500b64de.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3236"] :question/problem "The line $-4x+3y=24$ intersects the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter, find the equation of the circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/c8ed2363f6899e90eff46d8e45107ed5eee19582aeaed624c9831e5a128ff10a.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ and solve $-4x+3y=24$ for $x$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3(0) & =24 \\\\ -4x & =24 \\\\ x & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(0)+3y & =24 \\\\ 3y & =24 \\\\ y & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-6,0)$ and $(0,8)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{-6+0}{2}, \\dfrac{0+8}{2}\\right) = (-3,4).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-3,4)$ and a point on the circle, say $B(0,8),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{(0-(-3))^{2}+(8-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-3,4)$ and radius $r=5,$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-3))^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
